    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • XPENG Reveals G9L at the 2026 Paris Motor Show, Dubbed As the Ultimate \"Large SUV for the AI Era\"\nSix EVs Burn Down in Cadillac Dealership Inferno Amid GM Battery Recall\n13 photos\nPhoto: WFAA | YouTube\nSix Cadillac electric vehicles were completely destroyed, and two more were damaged after a massive fire broke out on the lot of a Cadillac dealership in Fort Worth, Texas. The blaze took the Fort Worth Fire Department exactly 17 hours and 33 minutes to completely extinguish.\nEmergency crews battled the inferno caused by the fire that originated from a single SUV parked in the yard of the Cadillac dealership. The fire soon spread to the neighboring vehicles, creating a highly complex and dangerous scene.\nFirefighters initially attacked the flames using standard water protocols before realizing they were battling multiple tightly parked EVs, so what would have normally been a 30-minute routine turned into a nearly 18-hour siege.\nCrew members deployed specialized EV fire blankets, which helped deprive the open flames of oxygen and hold the heat at bay so other nearby vehicles could be towed to safety. However, the trapped thermal energy caused several flare-ups and reignitions throughout the night.\nPhoto: WFAA | YouTube Fort Worth Fire Department decided to further monitor the vehicles for several days or even weeks, because damaged lithium-ion batteries can experience delayed chemical chain reactions and spontaneously reignite even after appearing entirely extinguished.\nVideos and reports from the scene indicate that most vehicles affected were Cadillac Escalade IQs. These vehicles are equipped with massive 205-kWh lithium-ion battery packs, which means that the firefighters were essentially dealing with over one megawatt-hour of concentrated energy storage.\nEV battery packs are intentionally sealed and watertight so that they can be protected against various challenges that may come up during daily driving. This design prevents water from easily reaching the internal cells. However, lithium-ion battery fire s burn significantly hotter and faster than conventional internal combustion engine fires.\nSix vehicles were declared total losses, and two others sustained minor exposure damage. Fortunately, no injuries were reported among dealership staff, customers, or first responders. The official cause of the initial fire remains under investigation.\nPhoto: WFAA | YouTube The fire occurred amid a current safety recall affecting certain 2026 model year General Motors EVs, including the Cadillac Escalade IQ , the Chevrolet Silverado EV, and the GMC Sierra EV, due to improper battery assembly components that can present fire risks.\nAccording to GM Recall Number N262562600 with the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA), a piece of equipment (a press machine) was misadjusted during scheduled maintenance. As a result, an internal high-voltage battery module component was not fully screwed down or secured in place, which can lead to puncture or damage, causing a short circuit.\nWhile the recall sounds alarming, it only affects exactly ten vehicles in the United States. All ten rolled off the production line of GM's Factory Zero line during a very tight window between May 4 and May 15. The affected models are the Chevrolet Silverado EV , GMC Sierra EV, Cadillac Escalade IQ, and GMC Sierra EV.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nCadillac Escalade IQ EV fire Cadillac General Motors General Motors recall",
